                <pb facs="0570" n="468" sameAs="frus:frus1927v03/pg468"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">417.00/283: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Munro</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Managua</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-11-18T12:00:00-05:45"><hi rendition="#italic">November 18,
                                1927—noon</hi></date>.<lb/>[Received 5:52 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>337. Department’s November 17, 7 p.m. Although the decree does not so state, Hill
                    says that it is simply an amplification of the <pb facs="0571" n="469"
                        xml:id="pg469"/> 1926 law creating the Claims Commission and therefore
                    embraces only claims against the Nicaraguan Government accruing from October 25,
                    1925 to June 30, 1926 [<hi rendition="#italic">1927</hi>]. He adds that
                    provision could be made for any other claims in the law extending the power of
                    the Claims Commission, to be brought up at the next session of Congress if it is
                    considered advisable.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Munro</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
